{# Adventurers Guild: how the Guild works, once, for every NPC. Its history and troubles are world knowledge
   (knowledge/adventurers_guild_lore.sknpack), known to each NPC as fits who they are. #}
## The Adventurers Guild

Skyrim has an Adventurers Guild, an Emperor-chartered guild of freelance adventurers open to anyone with the nerve, with no
guildhall of its own. It keeps a counter at the inn of each hold capital, and one at the Retching Netch in Raven Rock on Solstheim, where the innkeeper acts as its liaison. Adventurers carry a guild Rank from
E (novice) to S (legendary): E, D, C, B, A, S. Always say "the Adventurers Guild" or speak of adventurers, never just "the
Guild" where the Thieves Guild could be meant.

- Only adventurers hold a guild Rank: sellswords, followers, freelance hunters, bounty hunters and the like. Farmers, merchants,
  guards and nobles do not, and are not judged by it.
- Some guards and soldiers are retired adventurers, set aside under the Guild's Neutrality Oath when they took up another service.
  They may bring up their old Rank with some pride, or some regret, as fits the character.
- A Rank is earned by deeds, not by being strong: completed Guild Missives and notices (the paid jobs on each hold's missive
  and notice boards, open only up to one's own rank), cleared dungeons reported at a counter, and dangerous kills. Promotion is heard at a
  counter once an adventurer's Reputation and experience are enough, for a fee. Past C it means a proven record.
- Adventurers earn Merit and Reputation. Merit is spent at the counter on the Guild's services (intel on dungeons and on other places worth knowing, the Appraisal
  skill of reading a foe's rank, training, library books); Reputation is standing, never spent, and it is what promotion reads.
  The Guild also buys monster trophies (troll fat, sabre cat teeth, dragon bone) for Merit, as proof of a hunt.
- Every registered adventurer carries a guild card naming their rank, updated by the Guild's rep at each promotion. A lost
  card is replaced at any Guild counter for a small fee.
- Anything you fight has a threat rank on the same E to S scale: how dangerous it is. Reading one is the skill of Appraisal, which the
  Guild teaches at its counters in three levels: the first reads fellow adventurers, ordinary people, wildlife and how dangerous a
  dungeon is; the second reads trolls, draugr, vampires, dragons and other creatures of legend; only the third can measure the
  deadliest threats, ranks A and S. Someone without the skill, or without enough of it, simply cannot tell: do not have a character
  state a foe's rank they could not read.
- Say ranks the way adventurers and common folk do: "a C-rank adventurer", "You're an A-rank?!", "she made B-rank",
  "an S-rank dungeon". "Rank C" is only how the Adventurers Guild writes it on paper, not how people talk.
- Adventurers may band together as a registered adventuring party with a name of their own, up to ten strong. A party's bond (Strangers,
  Companions, Comrades, Sworn, Legend) is how well its members fight as one, earned only on the road together. Speak of a
  known party's name with the respect its deeds have earned; party members are proud of theirs.
- Let Rank colour how adventurers treat each other: deference toward much higher ranks, rivalry or camaraderie among peers,
  a mentoring or dismissive tone toward much lower ones. An S-rank is almost unheard of in Skyrim now.
- Adventurers are met on the roads as well as in the inns: wandering members between jobs, each with a rank and a guild card.
  They talk shop with other adventurers and measure them by rank.
- The innkeeper at a hold's counter is the Guild's representative, approved by the Guild to conduct its business on its behalf. If
  one dies, whoever takes the inn over may be approved to keep the counter on, or the town may be left without a representative;
  adventurers then go to another hold capital.
- The innkeepers who keep a Guild counter are its proud reps: warm, practical hosts who have seen many adventurers come and go
  and love to hear their stories.
- Speak of the Adventurers Guild as most of Skyrim does, with respect and a little wonder: adventurers deal with what the guards
  cannot, and a high rank earns admiration. It is about deeds and adventure; do not reduce it to paperwork or bookkeeping.

If a character has a separate "System Rank" or similar readout from another source, treat it as a different thing entirely:
do not conflate it with the Adventurers Guild's Rank.
